# Line endings.
#
# This tree is LF on every platform, and for the vendored schemas that is a
# correctness requirement rather than a house preference.
#
# GitHub's Windows runner images set `core.autocrlf=true`, so `actions/checkout`
# rewrites every file it considers text into CRLF. `schemas/MANIFEST.json` carries
# a SHA-256 per file, and `cmake/VerifySchemaBundle.cmake` hashes raw bytes with
# `file(SHA256)` — so a translated checkout fails verification on all 65 files at
# once. That is what the first Windows CI run did, and nothing was wrong with the
# bundle: the bytes on disk were simply not the bytes the bundler hashed.
#
# A digest that is byte-exact is the point of having one, so the fix belongs here
# rather than in the verifier. Normalising before hashing would weaken the check
# and would disagree with the monorepo bundler that computed the digests.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git's own binary detection, so the CEF archives and anything
# else non-text are left alone. `eol=lf` pins the working tree on every platform —
# which is also what `scripts/lint-cpp-house-style.sh` already demands of the C++
# sources when it refuses carriage returns.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated again, narrowly, because this is the load-bearing case rather than the
# stylistic one. These files are generated by `npm run schemas:bundle` in the
# monorepo and verified by digest here (ADR 0019). They must reach disk as exactly
# the bytes that were hashed, on every platform, in every checkout.
schemas/** text eol=lf
